sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: docs/cli/firewall/index.mdx; ADR 0244 and ADR 0245 (rules, admission); ADR 0271 and docs/reference/gateway-engine.md (execution).
  %% Engine flow: the kind runs on the Gateway state machine when ORBIT_ENGINE_KINDS lists it and the target Node's executor reported `ufw.ensure_rule`; otherwise it still runs the Swamp workflow operation-firewall-direct, which goes once every Node reports.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: firewall:allow
  %% kind: firewall.allow
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=executes tone=blue
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>state machine
  participant N as Target Node<br/>agent executor
  CLI->>GW: firewall:allow PORT<br/>--node=NODE
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit firewall:allow PORT --node=NODE`
  %%> - syntax: `<port> --node=ID [--from=SOURCE] [--protocol=tcp|udp] [--label=TEXT]`
  Note over GW: Validate, record the rule
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to the target (`node_access.required`); the target is Linux (`firewall.platform_unsupported`)
  %%> - the port, source, protocol and label pass validation; admission refusals create no rule or Operation and change nothing on the Node
  %%> - no other firewall change holds the target (`operation.target_busy`); one lock per Node, including the protected-access skip
  alt repeats protected access
    Note over GW: Skipped — already applied<br/>firewall.rule_exists
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - the ports lie inside one protected port or range Orbit opens, and the source is one that entry admits
    %%> - a range extending beyond that entry or a source it does not admit takes the operator-rule path
    %%> Result:
    %%> - records a succeeded Operation without an operator rule, order or Node contact; this branch uses recorded intent, not a UFW check
    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>succeeded Operation
  else operator rule
    Note over GW: Record step, start event<br/>ensure-0 · orbit.engine.start
    %%> Requirements:
    %%> - an opposite action for the same port, protocol and normalized source is refused (`firewall.rule_conflict`)
    %%> - a both-protocol rule beside a single-protocol rule on the same port and source is refused, for either action (`firewall.rule_overlap`)
    %%> Allowed:
    %%> - different source scopes may overlap; narrower sources take precedence regardless of action
    %%> - a repeat reuses the identity record and checks the Node in a new Operation; a label-only repeat updates the recorded label without rewriting correct UFW comments
    %%> Result:
    %%> - records the new or reused rule as provisioning, the full non-secret intent, and the engine step `ensure-0`
    %%> - publishes the start event on JetStream; the state machine takes it from there; nothing has happened on the Node yet
    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation ID
    GW->>N: Send the signed order<br/>ufw.ensure_rule · NATS
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - signs one order for the target Node: operation `<id>:ensure-0`, target the Node itself, so its firewall changes run one at a time
    %%> - publishes it to the Node's durable consumer on the ORDERS stream, over NATS on WireGuard
    %%> Inputs:
    %%> - `action`, `port`, `protocol`, `source`, `comment`, `ensure: present`, `retain`
    %%> Deadline:
    %%> - a JetStream schedule wakes the step after 10 s; with no receipt by then, a new order goes out; after 120 s the step fails with `engine.step_budget_exceeded`
    %%> - an order older than its 8 s expiry is refused as `expired`, and the Gateway sends a new one
    %%> Why the engine:
    %%> - measured on the nested fleet: 0.55–0.67 s per Operation, against 2.8–3.7 s on Swamp; a repeat takes 62 ms on the Node
    Note over N: Read the rules<br/>ufw status numbered
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - the executor checks the order's signature, then reads numbered UFW rows to plan insert positions and detect drift on a repeat
    %%> - inactive UFW fails with `firewall.backend_inactive` before mutation; never enables the firewall
    Note over N: Plan the changes
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - plans missing lines and moves misplaced lines; leaves identical, well-placed lines and their comments untouched
    %%> - only overlapping ports, protocols and source networks can make an existing line misplaced; unrelated managed trust does not force a move
    %%> - managed allows, including interface- and destination-scoped trust, do not force an existing allow to move: they cannot change its verdict; an overlapping deny still requires a move
    %%> - places new operator lines above Orbit's broad managed rules and narrower sources above broader ones; each address family is ordered separately
    %%> - plans one allow per selected protocol and applicable address family; a port range stays a range
    %%> - numbered positions account for earlier planned changes
    %%> How:
    %%> - the typed primitive `ufw.ensure_rule` in the executor (crate `firewall`, a port of the former Swamp planner)
    alt changes needed
      Note over N: Change the rules<br/>one ufw argv per change
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - runs the planned ufw commands one at a time; moves a misplaced line by deleting and reinserting it; preserves unrelated rules
      Note over N: Read the result<br/>ufw status numbered
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - collects the final rows for the one end-state check
    else already applied
      Note over N: Skipped — already applied<br/>firewall.rule_exists
      %%> Result:
      %%> - no mutation or second UFW read; the check uses the first read and must pass before a successful skip is reported
    end
    Note over N: Check the rules
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - compares every expected line and its narrow-first position
    %%> Failure:
    %%> - a missing or misplaced line fails with `firewall.verify_failed`
    alt rules present and ordered
      N-->>GW: Receipt: applied<br/>changes N
      %%> Result:
      %%> - the receipt goes to the RECEIPTS stream; the order is acknowledged only after JetStream stored it
      %%> - the state machine marks the step done and calls the kind's projection: the rule becomes active and the Operation succeeds
      %%> - `changes: 0` reports `Skipped — already applied (firewall.rule_exists)` in the successful result
    else read, apply or check fails
      N--xGW: Receipt: failed<br/>error_code
      %%> Result:
      %%> - the rule stays visible as failed and the Operation fails with `failed_step: ensure-0`; the rule records the `error_code` for `firewall:list`
      %%> On the Node:
      %%> - previously added lines remain, and a line deleted for reordering may be absent; no rollback
      %%> Retry:
      %%> - repeat after fixing the cause; reuse the record, keep correct lines and repair missing or misplaced ones without duplicates
    end
  end
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show OPERATION
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - asks for the Operation's status until it has ended; the steps list shows `ensure-0`
    GW-->>CLI: status + result
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the current status, and the result once the Operation has ended
  end
